      "generation_latency": 3.3439,
      "total_latency": 6.2004,
      "context_count": 4,
      "answer": "Paging works by dividing the physical memory into fixed-size pages. When a program attempts to access a page that is not currently stored in RAM (RAM), a \"page fault\" occurs, indicating that the requested data is not available. To resolve this issue, the operating system maps the virtual address space of the process onto the physical memory using these pages.\n\nPaging solves the problem of efficient use of secondary storage by allowing programs to access any part of their code or data without having to worry about where it physically resides in RAM. This eliminates the need for contiguous allocation of physical memory, which can be inefficient and wasteful. By mapping virtual addresses directly onto physical pages, paging optimizes resource utilization and improves overall system performance.",
